s testing actually mean  well testing in the essence is the  comparison of an unknown sample with  something known  and that something known is what we  refer to as the reference material  this means that the quality of your  reference materials  is directly affecting the quality of  your analytical result  as you can see here there are many  aspects of a product that can be tested  testing can be qualitative or  quantitative  qualitative tests typically confirm the  identification of an analyte or its  presence or absence in a sample  quantitative tests for example determine  the actual amount of an analyte in a  sample  or the potency of a drug  tests can focus either on the material  itself  for example its identity chromatographic  purity  content as mass percentage or physical  properties like melting point or  viscosity  other tests focus on the impurities  present in a material  which can be water residual solvents  organic or inorganic impurities  each of these different aspects of  testing require a specific reference  material  reference materials can be used in  different formats in the testing  laboratory  depending on product availability and  method requirements  the three formats for reference  materials are a need form  usually as a powder or liquid in  solution  or in matrix neat reference materials  are typically available in a screw top  vial  they can be used to make stock or  working solutions  often weighed and prepared daily or  weekly  the advantage of using neat reference  materials  is the big flexibility for different  applications  with larger material amount available  also many chemicals are more stable in  powder form  on the other hand there is an additional  handling step required by the end user  which can bear challenges especially if  the material is hygroscopic  viscose or toxic depending on the skills  there can thus be a greater weak to weak  variability  of the resulting working solution  reference materials in solution with  certified concentration and stability  will avoid these problems these are very  convenient to use and save a lot of  preparation time  especially for calibration mixtures  on the other hand you rely on the  availability of a solution mix  suitable for your need and have less  flexibility  matrix reference materials provide the  analytes in a matrix similar to the  sample analyzed  these are used to develop and validate  test methods  and to correct bias from the sample  preparation step  having certified matrix materials  commercially available  saves the time and effort to produce  in-house matrix materials  for example by spiking with an analyte  but the number of available matrix  materials is limited  so often there is no suitable product  available  also the analytes are often less stable  in a matrix  limiting the shelf life of these  products  i would like to introduce some metrology  terms now  a measurement is considered to be true  if the mean of all individual  measurements are close to the true value  as shown in the example on the left side  however the results here are widespread  meaning that the measurement is not  precise  and has a high uncertainty  the example on the right hand side shows  a precise test  with all individual results being close  together  however there is a bias in all these  measurements  so as a consequence this result is not  accurate  for analytical results in general and  reference materials in particular  we are aiming for accurate values  meaning that they are precise  so the reference materials should only  have a small uncertainty contribution to  the measurement  and also the value should reflect the  true value  this can be achieved by assuring  traceability  metrological traceability is an  important concept in the world of  reference materials  it means that measurements can be  meaningfully compared  across different places at different  times  by different people and using different  equipment  the measurement result must hereby be  related to a reference  through a documented and unbroken chain  of calibrations  tracing back to the si unit of  measurement  the international system of units or s-i  defines the seven units of measure as  the basic set  from which all other si units can be  derived  the two most common si units of measure  for traceability of reference materials  are kilogram and mole which we use in  our reference material certification  methods  and certificates of analysis  now with this basic metrological concept  in mind  let's take a look at the different types  of reference materials  as this hierarchy pyramid shows the  level of certification  and traceability requirements increases  the higher you climb up in the pyramid  at the top level we find the primary  standards  the primary measurement standards are  produced by national metrology  institutes like nist in the us or jrc in  europe  these authorized bodies are recognized  by their national governments  as the source of truth considered to  provide the highest level of accuracy  and traceability  in a reference standard  in the pharmaceutical world you have  primary compendium standards  produced by the pharmacopeias like usp  in the u.s  or the european pharmacopoeia  while not necessarily a government  entity  these pharmacopoeias work closely with  government agencies  and are legally recognized in many  countries  as providing the highest level of  accurate and traceable reference  standards for use in pharmaceutical  monograph testing  where national governments give  standardization to the top level  specific iso requirements provide  standardization for the next two levels  certified reference material and  reference material  these iso requirements include 17034  and 17025 as well as guide 31.  reference material producers must meet  these iso requirements  to manufacture crms or rms  within iso crms are considered to  provide the highest level of accuracy  uncertainty and traceability to an si  unit of measurement  rms fall one level lower as they are  manufactured and tested  to a level less demanding for both of  these quality grades  certificates of analysis must be  provided  and the information contained within is  defined by iso  also note that while reference materials  is a quality grade  it can also be used as a general term to  encompass  all quality grades within the pyramid  the quality requirements for the last  two levels  are defined by each individual vendor  rather than by a national government or  by iso  for the analytical standard grade  manufactured by our company  the product is manufactured within the  general iso 9001 quality  system requirements not specific to just  reference materials  a certificate of analysis is provided  with an analytical standard grade  reference material  it contains information such as product  identity  purity and an expiration date  generally the level of certification  varies from vendor to vendor  the lowest level is the research grade  or research chemical  these may be well characterized but are  not designed for the use as reference  materials  and not considered analytical products  let's take a deeper look at the  differences among a crm  rm and the analytical standards grade  there are four quality attributes  required for the property value of a crm  stability homogeneity across the lot  uncertainty and traceability to the si  unit of measurement and to an nmi  primary measurement standard if  available  the rm quality requirements include just  the stability and homogeneity attributes  for the property value  but no traceability or uncertainty is  required  for isoguide 31 all information and data  on crm or  rm quality attributes for the property  value  must be included in the certificate of  analysis  for an analytical standard there is no  property value  since the manufacturing and testing of  the reference standard  is not performed according to iso  requirements  specific to reference materials  in the absence of a property value the  focus is on the material itself  is the material sufficiently pure stable  and homogeneous  is the material fit for its intended use  since the c of a for analytical  standards crates  are not aligned to reference material  specific iso or compendial requirements  the information provided in them will  vary from vendor to vendor  iso quality requirements direct the  manufacture  testing and documentation of crms and  rms  iso 17034 accreditation  means that an official body recognizes  the producer's capability  to manufacture crms  an iso 17025 accreditation  means that the laboratory is officially  recognized to be competent in analytical  testing  the accreditation documents provide  guidance on how to manufacture a crm  but only if a company has been  successfully audited by an official  accreditation body  it is authorized to manufacture crms  in addition the isoguide 31 is intended  to help producers  to prepare clear and concise  documentation  to accompany a reference standard such  as the certificate of analysis  note that our company globally has four  different  sites holding an iso 17025  and iso 17034 double accreditation  to manufacture certified reference  materials  iso 17025 accreditation is also relevant  for testing labs  one important note here for accredited  testing labs  the use of a crm grade is mandatory  if commercially available  the iso and pharma worlds have different  definitions of traceability  compendial and pharmaceutical regulatory  guidelines  do not require traceability to the si  units  but to a primary compendial standard  as crms merck secondary standards have  both types of traceability  thus providing greater accuracy for  pharma monograph testing applications  and with that we will look how all these  requirements translate to the content of  a certificate  here is what informations you find on a  cfa of a certified reference material  obviously there has to be a certified  value  this can be the concentration for a  solution crm  or a mass fraction for a neat crm  the value has to be reported with an  uncertainty  which is taking all the contribution  factors into account  including stability and homogeneity of  the material  there needs to be a statement about the  traceability  mentioning the primary material tested  against  which for example can be an east srm  the certificate should provide  information about how the stability and  homogeneity of the material has been  determined  then the certificate would always state  an expiry date  the certified value is guaranteed for  the duration of the shelf life within  the given uncertainty  this slide gives an overview of the  different certificates of analysis  for the different quality grades  it is quite apparent that as the level  of certification increases  the amount of information required on  the certificate increases as well  for more information please refer to our  new  rm quality webpage which you can find on  sigmaaldridge.com  qualitygrades crm  after introducing the different types of  reference materials and formats  i'd now like to discuss how materials  are certified  with focus on organic chemical compounds  here are some of the best practices for  certification of a reference standard  it's important that the material is well  characterized for both  identity and purity  the best approach for identity is clear  identification by techniques  such as nmr and mass spectrometry  purity or potency does not include just  chromatographic purity alone  but also residual solvents water and  trace in organics  from the manufacturing or purification  process  all are required to obtain a complete  picture  of chromatographic and  non-chromatographic impurities in the  material  mass balance uses multiple techniques to  determine  identity purity and all trace impurities  whereas other approaches such as  quantitative nmr  and titration primarily we use one  technique  all approaches are useful for  determination of purity or potency  we are focusing on mass balance  quantitative  nmr and isotope dilution mass  spectrometry  for organic molecules we'll now take a  closer look at  quantitative nmr and mass balance  concept of qnmr involves a high  precision weighing step  of an internal standard and the sample  of interest  after weighing both of these components  into the same vial  a deuterated solvent is added and both  substances are dissolved  because only the ratio of analyte and  internal standard is relevant for the  calculation  the exact concentration of the solution  is not important  after the nmr measurement selected  isolated signals of the sample  and the internal standard are compared  which allows for the calculation of the  mass fraction  with a very high precision  traceability is achieved by using for  example a material from nist  or an east srm traceable internal  standard  qnmr is a primary ratio method  and impurities do not affect the result  after a comprehensive number of tests  from content determination and  homogeneity to stability  the crm is issued in accordance to the  iso 17034  17025 double accreditation  with a certificate provided according to  isoguide 31.  including the components that we have  been discussed previously  like traceability statement expiration  date  certified value expanded uncertainties  as well as the required signatures and  stamps from the accreditation body  now let's take a look at the mass  balance approach  like qnmr the mass balance approach  assigns purity content  which represents the true amount of the  material by weight  here we use a comprehensive panel of  tests  to get a 360 degree view of the molecule  mass balance is not a simple assay to a  similar standard  we perform a full characterization of  the molecule  determining the identity by multiple  methods  and the purity by multiple methods  we perform two to three chromatographic  purity tests  with different columns and detectors  like lc and gc  to ensure we are detecting all  impurities  for an accurate determination of the  mass fraction  no potential impurities can be ignored  so beside the chromatographic purity  the water content residue solvents and  inorganic impurities are measured  each technique includes its own  validated method for carrying out one  part of the full characterization  the formula below shows how all the  results from the different testing  techniques  are taken together to generate the  purity factor for assigning the content  or potency value  as mentioned previously traceability is  an important quality requirement for a  certified reference material  traceability for mass balance is  achieved through each of the individual  component tests  mass measurement in each component test  is traceable through balanced  calibrations to an si  unit of mass in this case the kilogram  other components are traceable through  instrument calibrations and system  suitability tests  using higher order standards  on this slide we have mass balance  certification examples  on a variety of natural product need  materials  many of these compounds are difficult to  isolate  purify and stabilize  as you can see chromatographic purity  alone  is not adequate to assign a total  content  in the case of catechin 3 gallate the  material has residual solvent and water  as well as trace inorganic content  some of it as hydrate and some from the  process of isolation and purification  these residual impurities must be  quantified  and accounted for in use of the material  even though these would not be seen by  hplc or gc  for laboratories preparing their own  in-house working standards  significant error may be introduced in  quantitative uses of the reference  material  without full characterization of the  neat material  so this concludes the technical part of  my presentation  let's now look at the reference  materials for food and beverage testing  food testing is clearly on the rise and  projected to grow  at an average annual growth rate of more  than six percent in the coming years  so who are the players in food testing  and what factors are driving food  testing  well there's a lot of media coverage of  food scandals  incident of food fraud mislabeling or  adulteration  academic research groups are  contributing in discovering such  incidents consumer awareness of food  safety issues are leading to political  pressure  and therefore to the establishment of  new regulations  and a stricter enforcement by  international bodies  like iso or efsa and national  authorities like the fda  the food industry itself also has a big  interest to avoid food scandals  to avoid reputation damage other factors  are emerging contaminants for example  due to climate change  new food trends like insects or cultured  meat  and new increasingly sensitive  analytical techniques  all these factors drive an increase in  food testing  and as we learned in the first slide  reliable test results  require reliable reference materials  our portfolio comprises more than 20 000  certified reference materials and  analytical standards  but how can you find the one you need  you could for example use the search  function on the sigmaaldrich.com web  page  after entering your search term you can  then refine your search results by  choosing the quality grade or the format  of your need  on the left hand column  another possibility is to browse our  product categories on the reference  materials main  page sigmaaldrich.com standards  a tip here when you click on one of  these categories  and then on one of the subcategories you  can again refine your choice  by defining quality grade or formats  to make it even more easy for you we  also set up some segment specific pages  for food pharma and environmental  testing  here you will quickly find the relevant  product groups and subpages  for the corresponding testing segments  let's have a look at the food segment  page  the most relevant product categories are  here organized  in nine tiles the first group is  representing our microbiology crms  this range consists of more than 100  hydro-soluble disks  with certified cfu levels for the most  common bacteria and microorganisms  under the brands vitroids and lenticules  the second group is pesticides where we  have a very comprehensive portfolio of  more than  1400 certified reference materials  reference materials and analytical  standards grade  the latter under the well-known  pesticide  biotoxins are including mycotoxins  marine toxins cyanotoxins or  alkaloids the portfolio of veterinary  drugs  includes antibiotics and hormones crms  and standards of the brand veterinal  the tab food identity testing  combines some niche product lines for  example dna standards for  animal and fish identity testing  botanical reference materials  or crms for genetically modified  organisms  other contaminants include heavy metals  packaging contaminants  and food dyes the remaining types  include physical properties food matrix  materials  and a type called food quality testing  that combines amino acids  lipids carbohydrates vitamins or  phytochemicals  we unfortunately don't have time now to  discuss all of these product groups in  detail  but i would like to highlight three  product groups of special interest  or where we have recently launched  exciting new products  three highlight topics are marine toxins  extractables and leachables or food  contact materials  and food matrix materials here it is  highlighted where you can find these on  the food segment page  marine toxins are mainly produced by  algae  but they can be very harmful to humans  when they accumulate  through the food chain these toxic algae  are growing exponentially in so-called  algae blooms  it is difficult to predict when and  where such  algae blooms occur furthermore  due to global warming these can also  spread now to areas  where they were previously unknown  the development of lcms to become  increasingly sensitive  is causing a shift from older biological  assays  to lcms to test these toxins  therefore reliable reference materials  are required  but there is a challenge for us  reference materials producers  the pure toxins have to be isolated from  algae  and are mostly only available in very  small quantities  so we are using a combined method of  quantitative nmr  and isotope dilution ms to certify these  compounds  we call micro qnmr  a few milligram are enough to prepare a  stock solution  analog code of this solution is  quantified by  quantitative nmr after that the stock  solution is diluted to the final  concentration of the crm  and filled in ampoules the stability  tests are then performed  by isotope dilution ms  using this method we have so far  launched 15 crm solutions  for example the native form but also an  isotope labeled crm of saxitoxin  hydrochloride  as a representative of the paralytic  shellfish toxins  or the more recently added orcadic acid  or groups like brevitoxins and pinot  toxins  i am showing only some examples of  different product groups here to  demonstrate the variety of chemical  structures these toxins have  the next highlight group are our  reference materials for extractables and  leachables  or for food contact materials  the term extractables and leachables is  mainly in use in the context of  pharmaceutical products and medical  devices  in the field of food the more common  term used is food  contact materials there are many  region or country specific regulations  for food contact materials these often  define  what chemicals are allowed to be used to  produce materials that come into contact  with food  they usually come with a positive list  of chemicals  but also defining maximum migration  limits for a lot of these  in addition also the occurrence or  formation  of non-unintentionally added substances  has to be controlled  so extractables and leachables and food  contact materials  do have a different regulatory  background  but a lot of the target analytes are  widely overlapping  still we are facing thousands of  potential contaminants  this means that the analysis is usually  not target specific  a big challenge is that no potential  contaminant  should be overlooked in the analysis  while there are thousands of potential  analytes  with very different structures  so for a reference material mix we would  like a broad range of analytes with  different physical chemical properties  to make sure that the analytical method  used is suitable to find a wide range of  chemical structures  also we wanted to focus on the most  commonly found  extractables and on the most toxic ones  to develop these products our internal  colleagues from the extractables and  leachables slab  from the central analytical services in  darmstadt  helped us to define the most useful  combination of analytes  for reference materials they would also  use internally  based on this collaboration we launched  two first screening mixes for  extractables and legibles  one with chemicals typical for lc and  another one for gc  extractable studies you can find these  products  under the url given here  the last product group i would like to  focus on is food matrix  materials these are real life samples of  different matrices  certified for the content of certain  analytes  such as mycotoxins in vine or heavy  metals in milk powder  or there are countless combinations  actually  materials can be used to develop or  validate analytical methods  including the sample preparation step to  correct for matrix effects  to make it easier to find a suitable  product among the more than 200 food  matrix reference materials in our  offering  on our web page you will find the  products listed by  analyte type or by matrix type  so if for example you are interested in  materials for fish  and seafood you can easily find all the  related matrix materials  that are available one very interesting  new addition  is a range of food matrix materials  certified for  elemental content of carbon nitrogen  hydrogen and sulfur  or for isotope ratio  these products are very well suited to  verify your results  in elemental analysis or isotope ratio  in food matrices to potentially detect  food fraud or adulteration  we are planning to further expand our  offering for food matrix materials  and offer a bigger variety of matrix  analyte combinations  for this we would appreciate the input  from our customers  so we can be focusing on the products  that are most needed by you  so if you could take five to ten minutes  and fill out a little survey on our  webpage  this would be very helpful for us thanks  a lot  i have been talking now all the time  just about reference materials  since this is of course the topic of  this webinar  but at last i also would like to point  out that our company is offering all  consumables  that you will need in your analytical  workflow under the sopelko brand  from sample preparation products like  spe  over to solvents analytical reagents  tlc plates or chromatographic columns  if you would like to be kept up to date  about new product additions  regulations or a lot of useful  applications  i would like to recommend you to  subscribe to our analytics reporter  magazine  which is issued about four times a year  and you can get either printed or also  as an online version by email  so please visit our webpage at  sigmaaldrich.com  analytics to subscribe  with that i have reached the end of my  presentation  i would like to say a big thank you to  the organizers of this event  and of course i thank you all for your  interest and your attention  thank you very much  and thank you dr knowledge for that  informative presentation  and we will now start the q a portion of  the webinar  remember if you have any questions you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on that ask the question box  located at the